  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Saturday)\nIssued at 125 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nMorning satellite imagery depicts the amalgamation of former\nIsaias and an upper trough over the central Appalachian Mountains\nmoving northeast. Drier air has managed to filter into the Mid-\nSouth behind this system, leading to clear and precipitation-free\nconditions throughout the region Sunday. Ample sunshine and light\nwinds will allow for warmer temperatures with highs expected to\nclimb into the upper 80s and low 90s through this afternoon with\nlows 60s.\n\nWarming temperatures will continue to build into the region\nthrough the early part of this upcoming week as upper ridging\nincreases in amplitude behind the trough. Highs are forecast to\nrise into the low 90s through the middle of the week. Weather\nwill be dry as well as no systems or notable surface boundaries\nmove through the region during this time. However, surface\nmoisture will increase with dewpoints in the upper 60s and low\n70s as southerlies return Monday and Tuesday, leading to less\npleasant heat than we have had recently.\n\nBy late Wednesday, the returning moisture will begin to interact\nwith a surface front just north of the region, potentially\nsignaling the next best chance for precipitation in the Mid-South\ninto Thursday morning. Medium-range guidance currently depicts an\naxis of MUCAPE (~1000 J/kg) stretching from central Oklahoma east\ninto the Ohio River Valley. Most of the instability looks to\nremain north of the Mid-South, leading to a lower coverage with\nsouthern extent and a decrease in any severe weather impacts. So,\nfor now expect isolated to scattered convection across the\nnorthern third of the region Wednesday evening through Thursday\nbefore the instability wanes.\n\nAn upper trough will begin to dig into the northern Plains and\nMidwest Friday with an accompanying surface cold front. Current\nguidance points to the air mass behind the cold front to be more\npolar in origin, advecting fall-like conditions across much of\nthe CONUS. The front is expected to reach the Mid-South Friday\nand will clear the region by Saturday morning with little to no\nprecipitation expected upon its arrival. Temperatures will then\ndrop with highs on Saturday in the 60s and low 70s amidst mostly\nclear conditions. Additionally, overnight temperatures in this\ntype of air mass will likely drop into the 40s in most areas with\nthe potential for areas across the Tennessee River Valley to drop\nbelow 40. In all, it appears increasingly likely that a pattern\nchange will bring a shot of truly fall-like weather into next\nweekend.",
